// One shape, built from a handful of named ones.
//
// An isosurface is arithmetic: a sum over a place in space, negative inside the thing and positive
// outside it, and the surface is wherever it crosses zero.  Written out in full, a shape like this one
// is a single expression a dozen lines long that nobody can read and nobody dares edit.
//
// Written this way it is four small functions and one line that puts them together.  Read the last
// line of the isosurface below and the shape is plain: four balls round the middle, joined to a ball
// on top and a stem below.  That is the whole point.  The arithmetic did not get simpler -- it is the
// same sum, and the renderer sees exactly the same thing -- but it acquired names, and names are what
// a person reads.
//
// Three of the functions are shapes and one is a way of joining shapes:
//
//   ball    is a distance out from a point, less a radius.  Negative inside.
//   rod     is the same about a line rather than a point, and then cut off top and bottom, which is
//           what `max` does: taking the larger of two sums keeps only where *both* are inside.
//   merged  runs two shapes together with a soft join, the softness being the one thing that says how
//           gently.  Left alone it is gentle; the joins here are set tighter, so the parts still read
//           as parts.
//
// Note that none of them works anything out on the way to its answer, and that is not a stylistic
// choice.  The first draft of `rod` named its distance-about-the-line before cutting it off, and the
// renderer refused it by name -- which is the rule below, met in the wild.
//
// **These fold bodily into the field.**  There is no calling going on when the picture is drawn: each
// function's body is folded in where it was called, values and all, and what the renderer compiles is
// the one big expression a person would otherwise have had to write.  That is why it costs nothing,
// and it is also the reason a function used here must be a single answer -- something with workings
// before it has no expression to fold, and an isosurface must be able to differentiate what it gets
// in order to know which way its surface faces.
//
// One thing worth noticing, since it is what makes any of this useful: `merged` is handed the *result*
// of other calls.  A value a folded function receives may itself be a piece of arithmetic rather than
// a settled number, so shapes can be built out of shapes.  Without that, a vocabulary could only ever
// be given plain numbers, and there would be little reason to name anything.
//
// Change `softness` in one place and every join in the picture softens together.  Change `ball` and
// every ball follows.  That is the difference between a shape that was written and a shape that was
// described.

context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    angles are degrees
    info {
        title 'A Shape Vocabulary'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'An isosurface built by folding a handful of named shape functions into one another.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

// A ball: how far out from a point, less how big it is.  Negative inside.
function ball(cx, cy, cz, radius) -> number {
    return √((x - cx)² + (y - cy)² + (z - cz)²) - radius
}

// A rod: the same about a line, then cut off above and below.  Taking the larger of two sums keeps
// only where both are inside, which is how one shape is cut against another.
function rod(cx, cz, radius, reach) -> number {
    return max(√((x - cx)² + (z - cz)²) - radius, abs(y) - reach)
}

// Two shapes run together.  The softness is the only thing that says how gently they join, and it has
// a fallback so the common case need not mention it.
function merged(first, second, softness = 0.12) -> number {
    return -log(exp(-first / softness) + exp(-second / softness)) * softness
}

camera {
    location [0, 2.0, -6.2]
    look at [0, 0.1, 0]
    field of view 42
}

background [0.05, 0.06, 0.09]

point light { location [-6, 8, -7]  color [1, 0.97, 0.92] }
point light { location [6, 3, -3]   color [0.30, 0.32, 0.40] }

plane {
    material {
        pigment checker { [0.30, 0.30, 0.34], [0.20, 0.20, 0.24]  scale 1.1 }
        specular 0.1
    }
    translate Y -1.15
}

isosurface {
    // The shape, said once: four round the middle, joined to one on top and a stem below.
    function {
        merged(
            merged(merged(ball(-0.62, -0.1, 0, 0.42), ball(0.62, -0.1, 0, 0.42)),
                   merged(ball(0, -0.1, -0.62, 0.42), ball(0, -0.1, 0.62, 0.42))),
            merged(ball(0, 0.62, 0, 0.46), rod(0, 0, 0.16, 0.95)))
    }
    bounded by [-1.4, -1.4, -1.4], [1.4, 1.4, 1.4]
    material {
        pigment [0.48, 0.64, 0.86]
        specular 0.55
        shininess 110
    }
}
